#
# D-144 — A SPAWN RECORD IS A CLAIM THAT AN AGENT EXISTS.
#
# THE HARM, driven through the real server._DISPATCH at d8215c5: a wave of
# three castings whose THIRD prompt will not decode. Foundry-Cast-Wave refuses
# — correctly, and before writing casting 3's record — but castings 1 and 2
# wrote theirs in earlier iterations of the same loop, so spawns.log holds
# `dispatched [1, 2]`. Aged past the 900s threshold, Foundry-Liveness then
# reports
#
#   agents          [('casting-1','no_ledger'), ('casting-2','no_ledger')]
#   needs_attention ['casting-1', 'casting-2']
#   detail          "casting-1 was dispatched from spawns.log 60 minutes ago
#                    and has written no progress ledger at all ... or it died
#                    before its first line. This row is the only trace of it
#                    either way."
#
# Two teammates that were never spawned, reported as teammates that may have
# died. CT-004 / AC-021 / OT-010 exist to tell a stalled agent from a slow one,
# and this is the worst answer available: a NON-EXISTENT agent reported as one
# that might be dead, whose only remedy is to go looking for it.
#
# THE WRITE SITE'S OWN COMMENT CLAIMED THE OPPOSITE, in as many words: "a wave
# whose third prompt will not decode must not have logged two spawns to
# spawns.log first." It logged exactly two. Refusing EARLY was never enough,
# because the earlier iterations had already written.
#
# THE FIX IS THE UNIT, NOT THE ORDER. The dispatch records are buffered while
# the castings are checked and appended ONCE, after every casting in the wave
# has cleared every check. `_append_spawn_records` is the only writer, so both
# spawn doors hold the property rather than agreeing on it, and the single
# door's own ordering is asserted separately below as the adjacent path.
#
# ALL THREE REFUSAL BRANCHES, not just the reported one. The loop refuses on
# undecodable, missing and empty prompts; the pre-existing test covered the
# decode branch alone, so a fix bound to it would have left the other two.
#
# AND THE LOCK IS WHAT MAKES "ALL OR NONE" TRUE AGAINST A READER, not only
# against a refusal. O_APPEND orders concurrent writers' bytes and says nothing
# about a Foundry-Liveness call arriving between the second and third record of
# a wave. The writer holds LOCK_EX for the whole payload and `_read_spawn_log`
# takes LOCK_SH around the read, so a concurrent reader waits and then sees the
# whole wave. `..._never_sees_a_partial_wave` drives that against a real lock
# holder: the reader is started while the log on disk is EMPTY, asserted to be
# still blocked, and only then is the wave written and the lock released — so
# what it returns is all three records and never the empty prefix it would have
# read had it not waited.
#
# The `phantom` rows assert the far end of the loop both ways: empty after the
# refusal, and all three castings on the roster after the same wave is really
# handed out. Emptiness that is not contrasted with a reachable non-empty is
# just an unreachable threshold.
